        Setting up Airflow as a Docker Container

1. Update Ubuntu Packages
>>sudo apt update
Refreshes the package repository list.

2. Install Docker
>>sudo apt install -y docker.io
Installs Docker engine.
-y → auto confirms installation.

3. Start Docker Service
>>sudo systemctl start docker
Starts Docker immediately.
>>sudo systemctl enable docker
Makes Docker start automatically after reboot.

4. Allow Ubuntu User to Use Docker Without sudo
>>sudo usermod -aG docker ubuntu
Adds user ubuntu to Docker group.
Without this, you must use:
>>sudo docker ps
After this change, you can use:
>>docker ps

5. Apply Group Changes
>>newgrp docker
Refreshes group permissions without logout/login.

6. Verify Docker Installation
>>docker --version
Example output:
Docker version 26.x.x

7. Install Docker Compose
Your command has a formatting issue.
Use this corrected command:
>>sudo curl -L "https://github.com/docker/compose/releases/latest/download/docker-compose-$(uname -s)-$(uname -m)" -o /usr/local/bin/docker-compose

Make it executable:
>>sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/docker-compose
Verify:
docker-compose --version

8. Create Airflow Project Folder
>>mkdir ~/airflow-docker && cd ~/airflow-docker
Creates a dedicated folder for Airflow.

9. Create Required Directories
>>mkdir -p ./dags ./logs ./plugins ./config
Folders meaning:
Folder	Purpose
dags	Airflow DAG files
logs	Task logs
plugins	Custom plugins
config	Configurations

10. Download Official Airflow Docker Compose File
>>curl -LfO 'https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/2.9.1/docker-compose.yaml'
Downloads official Airflow container setup.

11. Set AIRFLOW_UID
>>echo -e "AIRFLOW_UID=$(id -u)" > .env
Creates .env file.
Verify:
cat .env
Expected:
AIRFLOW_UID=1000
This prevents file permission issues.

12. Initialize Airflow Database
>>docker-compose up airflow-init
This:
Creates Airflow metadata DB
Creates admin user
Initializes setup
This step may take a few minutes.

13. Start Airflow Services
>>docker-compose up -d
Starts all containers in background.
Services include:
webserver
scheduler
postgres
redis
triggerer

14. Verify Running Containers
>>docker-compose ps
You should see multiple running containers.

15. Access Airflow UI
Open browser:
http://YOUR_PUBLIC_IP:8080
Example:
http://13.234.xx.xx:8080

Default login:
Username	Password
airflow	airflow

